    "summary": "Sudan has refused an entry visa to Redwan Hussein, director general of Ethiopia's National Intelligence and Security Service, who sought an urgent visit to the country, amid escalating tensions between the two neighbors.",
    "content": "Sudan has rejected a visa request by Redwan Hussein, the director general of Ethiopia's National Intelligence and Security Service, according to diplomatic sources cited by Al-Karama newspaper. Hussein had applied for an urgent entry visa through Sudan's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, but the Sudanese government declined to grant it.\n\nThe incident comes at a time of heightened tension between Sudan and Ethiopia, which have been at odds over multiple issues in recent years. The two countries share a long border and have clashed over the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam, the al-Fashaga farmland dispute, and cross-border security concerns.\n\nEthiopia has not publicly commented on the visa denial. Sudan's decision signals a further cooling of relations between the two governments, which have seen diplomatic engagement fluctuate since the outbreak of Sudan's civil war in 2023.\n\nThe move carries particular significance for the Somali diaspora, which closely follows developments in the Horn of Africa. Many Somalis have family, business, and security ties that span Sudan and Ethiopia, and any deterioration in relations between these two key regional states could affect trade routes, refugee flows, and stability in the wider region.\n\nSomali communities in the diaspora have historically relied on Khartoum and Addis Ababa as transit points and diplomatic partners. A prolonged diplomatic rift could complicate travel, remittances, and humanitarian access for Somalis affected by the overlapping crises in the region.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/sudan-oo-fiiso-u-diiday-madaxa-sirdoonka-itoobiya-oo-doonayay-booqasho-degdeg-ah/",
