    "title": "Puntland Maritime Forces Free Hijacked Vessel MTHonour Off Bandarbayla Coast",
    "original_title": "Ciidamada puntland oo soo furtay markab ay burcad-badeed ku qabsatay xeebaha Maamulkaas",
    "slug": "puntland-maritime-forces-free-hijacked-vessel-mthonour-off-bandarbayla-coast",
    "summary": "Puntland's maritime police force has rescued the fuel tanker MTHonour and its crew after pirates held the vessel for over five months off the coast of Bandarbayla.",
    "content": "Puntland's maritime police force, known as the PMPF, has successfully freed the vessel MTHonour, which had been hijacked by pirates and held off the coast of Bandarbayla in the Bari region. The operation took place on September 29, 2026, according to reports from Puntland. The MTHonour, a fuel tanker sailing under the flag of Palau, was reportedly carrying cargo for traders based in Puntland.\n\nThe pirates seized the vessel on April 21, 2026, while it was en route to Mogadishu. For more than five months, the ship and its crew were held captive. The PMPF launched a planned operation to secure the vessel and rescue the sailors on board. Details regarding the condition of the crew or any injuries have not yet been released.\n\nThe hijacking underscores the persistent threat of piracy in the waters off Somalia, despite years of international counter-piracy efforts. Puntland's maritime force has been active in combating such crimes, but the incident highlights the challenges of protecting vast stretches of coastline. The MTHonour's seizure is one of several recent cases that have raised concerns about a possible resurgence of piracy in the region.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, particularly those with ties to Puntland's business community, this event is significant. The vessel was carrying fuel for local traders, and its hijacking would have disrupted supply chains, potentially affecting prices and availability of goods. The successful rescue operation may restore some confidence in maritime security, but it also serves as a reminder of the risks faced by those involved in trade and shipping in Somali waters.\n\nThe PMPF's intervention reflects ongoing efforts by regional authorities to assert control over their territorial waters. However, the duration of the hijacking—over five months—suggests that challenges remain in responding swiftly to such incidents. The international community has often supported anti-piracy measures, but the incident may prompt calls for renewed attention to maritime security off Somalia's coast.\n\nAs of now, further details about the operation and the fate of the pirates involved have not been disclosed. The Somali diaspora will be watching closely for updates on the crew's well-being and the impact on local trade. This event highlights the interconnectedness of security and economic stability in the region.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/ciidamada-puntland-oo-soo-furtay-markab-ay-burcad-badeed-ku-qabsatay-xeebaha-maamulkaas/",
