    "id": "news-1790673517050-11",
    "title": "Somali Customs Revenue Falls Nearly 40% Amid Hormuz Disruptions, President Says",
    "original_title": "Dakhliga Kastamka Soomaaliya oo Hoos U Dhacay Ku Dhawaad 40% Sababo la Xiriira xirnaanta Marinka Hormuz",
    "slug": "somali-customs-revenue-falls-nearly-40-amid-hormuz-disruptions-president-says",
    "summary": "President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud told the UN General Assembly that disruptions at the Strait of Hormuz have sharply reduced Somali customs income, dealing a blow to government finances.",
    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud has reported that customs revenue in Somalia has fallen by nearly 40 percent, attributing the decline to disruptions at the Strait of Hormuz. Speaking at the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly, the president said the obstruction of commercial shipping and trade flows through the strategic waterway has directly affected both government income and the broader Somali economy. The Strait of Hormuz, a narrow passage between the Persian Gulf and the Gulf of Oman, is a critical route for global petroleum and cargo shipments.\n\nThe 40 percent drop in customs collections comes at a time when Somalia is heavily dependent on imported goods for its domestic market. The president's remarks to the UN body underscored how instability in distant maritime corridors can quickly translate into fiscal pressure for countries along the Horn of Africa. The Somali government relies on customs duties as a major source of public revenue, and a shortfall of this magnitude threatens budget planning and public services.\n\nAccording to the president, the disruption has slowed the movement of goods and vessels, with ripple effects on trade and government finances. He did not specify the exact period over which the revenue loss was recorded. The Strait of Hormuz has seen repeated interruptions in recent years, often linked to regional tensions, and such closures can raise shipping costs and delay deliveries for import-dependent economies.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the revenue decline carries particular significance. Many diaspora members support relatives through remittances and invest in businesses that depend on imported commodities such as food, fuel, and construction materials. A sustained drop in customs income could weaken the government's ability to provide security and basic services, potentially affecting the environment in which diaspora-funded projects operate. It may also impact the prices of goods that families back home rely on.\n\nPresident Mohamud's address at the UN General Assembly placed Somalia's economic vulnerability in a global context, highlighting how events far from its borders can shape domestic stability. The Somali government has not announced specific measures to offset the shortfall, but the issue is likely to feature in upcoming fiscal discussions. How authorities respond will be closely watched by international partners and diaspora communities alike.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/dakhliga-kastamka-soomaaliya-oo-hoos-u-dhacay-ku-dhawaad-40-sababo-la-xiriira-xirnaanta-marinka-hormuz/",
